  "question": {
    "text": "A sinusoidal voltage <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mfenced><mi>t</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>210</mn><mi>sin</mi><mn>3000</mn><mi>t</mi></math> volt is applied to a series LCR circuit in which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mH</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#956;F</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#937;</mi></math>. The phase difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>&#934;</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;between the applied voltage and resultant current will be&nbsp;"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>tan</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>",
